"""Shared gameplay meaning, with precise machine-readable transport conventions."""
INSTRUCTION_BRIEF = 'Mark the switches that hold the light on—and can turn it off. Leave no extra marks.'
EXPLANATION_RULES = 'Mark ONE set of recorded switches that passes BOTH tests. HOLD: keep the marked switches at their recorded positions; the target light must be on at the target moment however the unmarked switches are set. CHANGE: keep all unmarked switches recorded; some setting of the marked switches must turn the target light off. Leave no extra marks: removing any marked switch must break at least one test. Different successful minimal sets can have different sizes; any one is accepted. CHANGE need not flip every marked switch. A marked switch refers to its recorded position, including zero. Follow the visible tracks from the starting pulse: a switch sends the pulse along its 0 or 1 branch; copies with the identical full label are one input at one moment and always change together; the same channel at different moments is a different input. Every successful final branch leads to the same target lamp; failure branches lead to a separate unlit exit. The golden path shows the recorded pulse, not a distinct goal. Intermediate output indicators are not additional target lamps. The board is a complete mechanism, not a hidden rule to discover. Replaying the recorded pulse provides no new experiment. Shared demonstrations illustrate the two tests and extra marks. Submit {"cells": [integer IDs]} once; use only the IDs in board.switches, which identify the visible playable switches. If no setting of any switches can turn the target light off, submit {"verdict":"no_explanation"}; this differs from an empty marked set. The public machine is an equivalent technical encoding of the board, available to both participants. Input cell ID = number_of_inputs * zero_based_step + zero_based_input_index; input indices follow machine.inputs. In transitions[state][v], v = sum(input_value[j] * 2**j). Each step emits the current-state/current-input transition output, then updates state. Earlier lights can turn off; only the target moment is judged. The target is output 1 at target_step. The limit is 480 seconds from server start, including delivery and interaction. Reasoning permits no simulator, calculator, code, web or outside tools; humans may use blank scratch paper. The probe condition permits only the supplied simulator, at most eight times. One final submission; malformed or late answers are incorrect. You may pause before starting the next item.'
MODE_RULES = {'explanation':EXPLANATION_RULES}
TUTORIAL_EXPLANATION = 'Each listed set passes HOLD and CHANGE, and no proper subset passes both. Any ONE set is correct; combining sets can leave extra marks.'


def rules_for_limits(mode, limits):
    """Use the session's declared budgets in both participant modalities."""
    rules = MODE_RULES[mode]
    rules = rules.replace('480 seconds', f"{limits['seconds']:g} seconds")
    if limits['probes'] not in (0, 8):
        rules = rules.replace('at most eight times', f"at most {limits['probes']} times")
    return rules
